To find the coefficient of the $x^{-2}$ term in the expansion of $(x^{3}-\frac{2}{x})^{6}$, we can use the binomial theorem. 
The binomial theorem is given by $(a+b)^n = \sum _{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}a^{n-k}b^{k}$
We observe that the term that will give $x^{-2}$ must be $\binom{6}{4}(x^3)^{2}(-\frac{2}{x})^{4}$.
$\binom{6}{4}(x^3)^{2}(-\frac{2}{x})^{4}$ = $15 x^6\frac{16}{x^4}$
